David Rose and his orchestra, starring Red Skelton.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.120
- Thank you very much, ladies and gentlemen,

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.980
for allowing me to come into your homes

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.040
and visit with you tonight.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.380
A lot of things have been happening.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:50.280
I read in the newspaper.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.240
I thought it was awful nice what Arthur Godfrey did.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.310
Took the heat off of John Wayne.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:02.810
Yeah, I knew they'd get nothing, but...

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:04.850
Yeah, I've had a lot of fun this week.

00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:05.910
I went down to the beach.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.420
Oh, and while I was down there, I saw my two friends,

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.480
Heathcliff and Gertrude, see?

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.680
And it seems that this week they went over to the valley.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.460
They flew over to San Fernando, see?

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.340
They're flying along, and finally they get up to the house

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:20.100
to where they're going to, the chicken farm.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.880
And they knock on the coop, and one of the...

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:25.980
One of the chickens came to the door, and he says,

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:26.870
Yes?

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.000
And he clipped it.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.940
says, uh, "Is Thelma here?"

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:34.930
Says, "Haven't you heard?"

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:36.030
She says, "You know what?"

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.820
She says, "She's gone.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.300
Been laying eggs for five years,

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:42.700
and she died from embarrassment.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.700
She found out she was a rooster."

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.070
So Gertrude says, "Well, um, is Ida here?"

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:55.160
He says, "No, show up.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:56.030
She's gone, too.

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:57.450
You know how she was?

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.640
She had a very peculiar way about her,

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.260
She'd put a color in front of her and she'd lay that color egg.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.850
Now a blue car would go by, she'd lay a blue egg.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:08.650
A red car goes by, she'd lay a red egg.

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:10.690
She's gone now.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:13.190
Somebody hung a crazy quill on the clothesline.

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.330
She's stripped gears trying to make good.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:22.290
By the way, the music that was played when I first came out

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:25.370
was played by David Rose and his orchestra.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:30.000
To me, I think David Rose is one of our most brilliant musicians, conductors,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.000
and composers.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:32.500
Now, a lot of people don't know this,

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.840
but he got his start with Phil Spittownley's

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.120
all-girl orchestra.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.500
He used to play piano under the name of Rose David.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:47.560
I used to be a musician myself.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.060
I love music, 'cause I don't know too much about it.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:53.690
I used to play the violin until after my first recital.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:55.130
And one of the critics came out and says

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:57.040
that it's the first time that the musician

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:00.000
had more guts than the violin.

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:05.150
But music really plays a very important part in our lives.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:06.730
I went to the opera the other night

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:08.910
and I said to my wife, "We saw Carmen."

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:11.090
And I said, "I thought the snow scene was wonderful."

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.210
And she says, "There's no snow scene in Carmen."

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:16.050
And then I realized I was sitting behind the popcorn machine.

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:18.920
And there was a lady came out to sing.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:20.420
She was a mess of superhero.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:25.100
I mean, she was a mess of something, I know that.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:27.400
And now she came out to sing and opera,

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:30.000
I don't think half the people understand it myself.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.700
I mean, I talked to Lawrence Milkior once,

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.180
and he said that one time he sang four pages

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.000
of the Santa Fe timetable,

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.700
and no one ever knew the difference.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:42.540
He says, "It's the first time that anybody ever applauded

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.040
the fact that number six didn't stop at Fresno."

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:49.960
Music is really, I get a big sick out of it myself.

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:53.500
I mean, I like to watch the way different people react to it.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:55.640
Now, the other night I went to a concert,

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:58.100
and I noticed one fellow who seemed rather bored.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:03:59.830
Wait, I'll show you what I mean.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:10.210
- One of these guys. - One of these guys.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:15.500
Oh Jack Webb, you've never seen those old timers that like sentimental music?

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:23.670
The guys that hang around Toot Shores, oh boy, one of these type of guys, four in the morning,

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:29.670
oh there's a nice record, Irish music.

